{
  "contract": "analysis",
  "version": 1,
  "description": "The analysis screens, by kind. opportunity: computeOpportunityCost — what each euro contributed would be worth had it bought the benchmark on the same Madrid day, at the benchmark's close of that day or the last one within 7 days before it (the latest close of a day wins), after bringing the benchmark's closes to euros at the rate of their own day and today's prices at today's. A purchase with no close is set aside as truncated (before the first close), stale (after the last) or gaps, by its cost. Units received in kind and swap legs are not contributions; sales add their net proceeds to the real value. An open position with no price is left out and named. Lines are ordered by difference, largest first, then by instrument id. realMwr and benchmarkMwr are XIRRs as in returns.json (the same algorithm, to six places), each over its own flows plus its value at now. ter: computeWeightedTer — value-weighted annual fee over the lines that have one, to six places; annualCost is the weighted sum to the cent; a line with no TER is excluded and named, a line worth nothing is skipped. rebalance: computeRebalance — weights are normalised to sum to one; each line's deficit is its weight of (held + contribution) less what it holds, floored at zero; the contribution is split by deficit, rounded half up to the cent, and the cent left over goes to the largest amount (the first of equals); drift is |share − weight| to six places. Shared with the mobile client: both implementations must reproduce every case. Events are LedgerEvents; an absent fxSource is IDENTITY and an absent funding is CASH. expect is written by running the TypeScript core; byHand.figures, where a case has it, are worked out by hand, its arithmetic says how, and each key is a dotted path into expect.",
